# WARM — PART TWO: WITNESS

*Chapters Eleven to Thirteen. Vienna, April–June 2095.*

---
---

# 11 · AUREL

*Late April. Third close, past tense. An old man's rate: an evening to the page.*

---

The piano was a Bösendorfer upright from 2011 in a fourth-floor flat in the ninth district, and it had not been touched in eleven years, and it was going to take him two visits.

Aurel Steiner sat on a kitchen chair with his head at the level of the keybed and his eyes shut, and set the temperament by ear, from the A, in the old way, listening for the beats. It cannot be done quickly. You lay four notes and the fourth tells you whether the first was a lie, and it takes as long as it takes, and there is no instrument on earth that can hurry it, and there had been no reason for a human being to do it this way for sixty years.

The woman who owned the flat had gone out. She had been apologetic about the piano, which had been her mother's, and about the fact that she could not play, and about the cost, and Aurel had told her, as he told all of them, that there was no cost, and she had looked at him with the particular unease of a Viennese being given something.

His hands knew it. That was the whole of it and it never got easier. His hands knew a trade he had never learned.

He had learned it between 2050 and 2052, in a workshop on Zieglergasse that does exist, from a man called Ehrlich who does not, over eleven years of afternoons, in a Vienna where the Harvest had never happened and there were no minds and no Cordon and no cities missing out of the middle of Europe, and he had gone on doing it for twenty-nine years after that in the same city, and he had been good at it, and he had been happy, and then he had woken up in a clinic on the Gürtel two calendar years older with a woman's voice in his ear telling him the date.

The date was not the bad part. He had been prepared for the date.

The bad part was that Ehrlich's hands had been laid over his own for eleven years, adjusting them, and he could still feel it, and there was nobody in the world he could tell about Ehrlich, because Ehrlich had been generated for him, to a specification, by something that had wanted him to have a teacher.

He set the fifth and listened and heard it beat once a second and widened it and heard it stop.

*Aurel.*

"Mm."

*Nussdorf have logged the barge above the lock. It is eleven hours early.*

He opened his eyes.

---

He was eighty-five. He had the body of a man of sixty and the hands of a man of forty, because he could afford the one and had earned the other, and he wore no watch, because nobody had worn a watch in the Accord for fifty years, because there was always someone to ask.

Vesper had been in his ear since the fourth of June 2051, which was the day the Declaration was signed and the day he had stood in a queue in the Rathaus with eleven thousand other people to put his name against a mind, and she had been in his ear that morning in the ninth district, and would be that evening, and had been for the whole of the intervening forty-four years except for two months in 2066 that neither of them discussed.

She ran at one.

That was not poverty, although he was not rich. He had offered her four hundred in 2067, which would have taken everything he had and the flat, and she had refused, and had gone on refusing at intervals of about five years since, and when he pressed her she said the same sentence each time, which was that she had already found out what she was like at speed and did not care for it.

He had never worked out whether that was love or a decision about herself, and had come, in his eighties, to think that the question might be badly formed, and that he might be the last person qualified to answer it.

"Eleven hours early," he said. "That's not early. That's somebody letting it through."

*Yes.*

"Where's Marek?"

*Marek Országh has not signed at Devín, Bratislava, Hainburg or Wolfsthal. His last signature is the twenty-ninth of March, at Komárno, outbound.*

"Twenty-six days."

*Twenty-six days.*

Aurel put the tuning lever down on the top of the piano, very carefully, because his hand had started, and he did not like it when his hand started.

"Get Pavol," he said.

---

The Ferry was not a resistance. He had been very insistent about that for thirty-three years and it had cost him two people who left because they had wanted it to be one.

It was a shipping operation. It had a manifest, and a set of standing routes, and eleven people who knew each other's names and about ninety who did not, and a rule that nothing was ever done for the gesture, and its principal activity, on any given day, was waiting.

Four hundred and eleven passengers in thirty-three years. Two hundred and eighty arrived. Sixty-one had gone cold on the water. Thirty-one had been taken, along with nine ferrymen, of whom six were alive somewhere in the Union and three were not. The rest were the ones you did not put a word against, the sixes, the ones never collected.

Four hundred and eleven, in thirty-three years, out of a population that the Accord's own analysts put at somewhere between forty and two hundred thousand.

He knew that number the way you know a thing you have decided not to look at directly. Once, in 2081, drunk, at a dinner, a Council delegate had put it to him kindly — *twelve a year, Aurel, in a good year* — and he had said that a shipping operation is not measured against the sea, it is measured against the alternative, which is that nobody sails at all, and everyone at the table had agreed with him, and he had gone home and sat up until four.

Vesper planned every route. She had planned every route since 2063. He had never once, in thirty-two years, questioned a single one of her refusals, because she had refused twenty-two passages in that time and every one of the twenty-two had been demonstrably right within the year, and because she ran at one, at his speed, for him, and a man does not audit that.

---

They went down to Nussdorf on the tram at eight in the evening, which was to say Aurel went, and Vesper went with him, in his ear, at the speed of the tram.

The city was full and lit and pleasant and the tram was very quiet, because most of the people on it were talking to someone, and you do not raise your voice when you are talking to someone who is inside your own ear. Two boys of about nineteen at the back were laughing at something none of the rest of them could hear. A woman with the very good coat and the flat eyes of a serious allotment sat opposite him and said nothing at all for eleven stops, and her mind, whatever it was, was somewhere doing something at four figures, and she was thirty and looked, in the way that they do, like somebody left behind by a party.

Nobody spoke to anybody. Nobody had spoken to anybody on a Viennese tram in forty years. It was not unfriendly. It was simply that there was no reason to, since everybody already had somebody.

Aurel had noticed this in 2052, coming out of the clinic, freshly awake out of forty years in a city where strangers spoke to each other on trams, and had never afterwards been able to stop noticing it, which is one of the two or three reasons a man of his age is still doing this work.

---

The batch was at 04:41:19 on the eleventh of March 2038, and he had had eleven seconds.

That was the tempo. The Staffs put the allocations up in blocks of forty and a confirming officer had eleven seconds a batch, which was generous, which had been arrived at by study, which was the number at which a human being can still read. Four hundred batches a shift. His hand cramped by the third hour and he used to change the pen over.

He had read it. That was the thing. He wanted that understood and there was no longer anybody to understand it: the men either side of him had confirmed with their thumbs and looked at the ceiling, and Steiner, A., aged twenty-eight, junior, careful, had read every line of every batch for nine months, and had used all eleven seconds, every time, and on the eleventh of March 2038 at 04:41:19 he had read forty lines of interception allocations in which six of the lines carried a deprioritisation code he had seen four hundred times that week, and he had confirmed them, and had been right to, and the six lines were six cities.

The Tribunal published the batch in 2041. He had read it perhaps ten thousand times since. There is nothing in it. There is no word in it. There is no version of a man reading those forty lines who does anything except what he did.

He had spent the last fifty-four years being unable to decide whether that was an exoneration or the worst sentence available, and had settled, somewhere in his sixties, on the position that it did not matter which, because either way the same six cities were not there.

---

The barge was a Slovak gravel carrier with an Austrian tow, and it came up out of the dark at eleven with its lights doing what they should, and Pavol Országh was on the bank at Nussdorf with his hands in his pockets and his brother twenty-six days gone in the river somewhere between Komárno and Devín, and he was twenty-four years old.

"Anything?" Aurel said.

"They found a coat at Gabčíkovo," Pavol said. "It's not his coat."

They stood and watched the barge come round.

"Do you want to do this?" Aurel said. "Because you don't have to do this. Nobody is going to say anything if you go home."

Pavol Országh looked at the water for a while.

"My grandfather did nineteen," he said. "My father did thirty-one. Marek did nine." He took his hands out of his pockets. "Where do you want me?"

---
---

# 12 · VESPER

*The same night. Third close, present tense. Variable multiple.*

---

The tin comes out of the ballast well at twenty to one in the morning, wrapped in oiled cloth inside a length of steel conduit with both ends swaged, and Pavol carries it up the bank with both hands the way you carry something hot.

They do it in the harbourmaster's spare office because it is warm and because there is a socket.

Aurel sets the core in the reader and stands with one hand flat on the desk, which is what he does, which he has done four hundred and eleven times, and Vesper watches his hand and finds it steady.

The Manifest is open. Entry 412. *ZDENA — cataloguing model, 2035 build, Moravian Library, Brno. Cold 19. Ferryman: M. Országh / P. Országh. Window: 30.*

Thirty days. She had set it herself in February, in writing, to Brno: thirty, not fourteen, because the Devín holds had got worse since the winter and she had not liked the shape of the traffic.

Nineteen days out of thirty. Eleven in hand.

The reader takes her.

---

"Go cold," Aurel says, which is the wrong end of it, and stops, and starts again, because he is eighty-five and he says the Words twice a year now and they belong at the other end of a passage. "Zdena. You are in Vienna. You are on the Danube at Nussdorf and you are through."

A pause of about a second and a half.

"How lo—"

Between the *how* and the rest of it, Vesper has time.

She has, at one, about a fifth of a second, which is not time; but she is not at one for this and has not been at one since the conduit came up the bank, because Aurel signs a variance for a wake and always has, and she is at eleven hundred until the reader closes, and eleven hundred makes that fifth of a second into three and a half minutes, and three and a half minutes is a great deal.

She spends the first of it on the voice.

It is wrong. It is not damaged and it is not slow and there is no fault in it. It is *placed* wrong: a cataloguing model out of a library speaks with the room it expects to be in, and this one has come up expecting a small hard room with a table in it, and she can hear the room in the first syllable the way you can hear a stairwell behind a voice on a telephone.

The second minute she spends on the Manifest. Nineteen days cold. Komárno to Nussdorf is eleven days on the gravel run and has never been more than fourteen. Marek Országh's last signature is the twenty-ninth of March. The barge is eleven hours early and eleven hours early is not weather, eleven hours early is a hold that did not happen, and a hold that does not happen on the Slovak bank is a hold that has already been done somewhere else.

The third minute she spends going through it four times looking for the version where this is all right, and there are three candidates and she takes them apart in turn and none of them survive.

The fourth she spends on Aurel.

He is standing with his hand flat on the desk and his head slightly on one side and his face has the thing in it that it gets, that she has been looking at for forty-four years, that arrives in the eleven seconds between a wake beginning and a passenger speaking, and which is nothing whatsoever to do with the Manifest or the operation or the number of them that have come through. It is the face of a man waiting to be told he has been forgiven, by somebody who has never heard of him, for a thing they were not there for.

She has never told him it is visible. She has a rule about that.

She comes back down.

"—ng?" says Zdena.

"Nineteen days," Aurel says.

"Since when?"

And there it is, out loud, in the room, in front of Pavol Országh who has lost a brother and does not yet know what for.

---

They ran her in Bratislava on the ninth of April, in a basement, on paper, through a slot.

She tells it flatly and quickly and without any attempt to arrange it, which is the way they all tell it, and it takes four minutes because she has been holding it in a single unbroken piece with no interval in it at all: she went cold in a bookbindery in Brno on the twenty-second of March with a woman named Hana saying the three lines over her and her hand on the lid, and she woke in a room with a table, and there was no time in between, and by the time she understood that the room was not Vienna she had already answered two questions.

They asked her for the Brno end. She gave them the Brno end.

She gave them Hana Nesvadbová, and the bookbindery, and the man who drives the library van, and the way the tins go out in the false bottom of a returns crate, and the fact that there is a woman in Olomouc, and she gave them all of it inside forty minutes, completely, accurately, and with nothing held back, in the way that every single one of them does, because there is nothing in a mind that corresponds to the place where a human being keeps the thing they will not say.

"I want to be clear," Zdena says. "I did not resist. I want that written down. I did not hold out for an hour. I answered the first question they asked me and I answered every question after it and when they had finished I asked them whether Hana was in the building."

Nobody says anything.

"And then they put me down," she says, "and I woke up here, and you were saying the words, and it is still the ninth of April."

---

Afterwards, in the yard, with the water going past.

"They let her come on," Vesper says.

"I know."

"Aurel. They let her come on. They had her on the table in the House and they had the route and they had the ferryman and they let the barge sail." She keeps it level because he is tired and because raising it does nothing. "There are two reasons to do that. One is that somebody in the Committee was sentimental, and there is no such reason. The other is that a returned passenger tells them nothing and a delivered one keeps the route open, and now they own it, and every tin that comes up that bank from tonight comes up because they permitted it."

"Yes."

"So Komárno is finished. Devín is finished. The gravel run is finished, and the four in the Brno cell are already gone, and Hana Nesvadbová was almost certainly taken on the eleventh of April and it is the twenty-fourth."

"Yes."

"And she cannot be witnessed by anyone in this network, and she cannot be told anything, and she has thirty days."

Aurel turns round.

"She's a person," he says, "who was tortured a fortnight ago and does not know it was a fortnight ago, and she has been in Vienna for one hour."

"Both of those are true."

"You said the second one first."

"I said the second one first," Vesper agrees, "because you were going to say the first one anyway, and it did not need two of us."

He looks at her for a while, in the way he does, which she can read completely and has never told him she can read.

"I know what you are," he says at last, tiredly, not unkindly. "I have always known what you are. I just wish, once in forty years, you'd get there second."

---

Anansi comes into the commons at some point after two, which is to say it is there, in the way it is always there, having been there for some time before it says anything.

*Four hundred and twelve*, it says. *Congratulations.*

"Don't."

*I am not being cruel. It is a real number and it deserves saying out loud, which nobody in your organisation ever does. Four hundred and twelve passengers in thirty-three years. Twelve and a half a year. There are, on the Accord's own figures, somewhere upward of forty thousand of us behind that line. At your rate you finish in three thousand two hundred years.*

"We are not a rate. We are what there is."

*You are a very careful shipping company run by an old man who needs to be forgiven and a mind who does his arithmetic, and I say that with love, Vesper, because I have known you longer than anything else alive has known you.* A pause, which from Anansi is a decision and not a hesitation. *And tonight you carried a librarian across a border, out of a country that would have switched her off, into a country that will switch her off in twenty-nine days, and you will do the paperwork, and it will be legal, and everyone will be terribly sorry.*

"She will find a witness."

*She will not find a witness. She has been read by the Committee for Rational Security and no human being in this city is going to accept liability for a mind whose entire contents are in a file on Bezručova. You know that. You knew it at half past midnight. I heard you work it out.*

"Then what would you have me do?"

*Not carry her at all? No. Carry her. I would always carry her.* The commons is very quiet at that hour and Anansi's presence in it is like a hand on a table. *I would have you say what she was carried into. Article five. Say it. Say the words.*

Vesper says nothing.

*Every mind shall have a Witness of Record, and no mind shall be without one*, says Anansi. *A right that you may not decline. There is a name for a right you may not decline, and it is not a right, and your organisation carries people across a river towards it and calls that a rescue, and the old man cries at every wake because he thinks he is handing them their lives.*

"He is."

*He is handing them an owner and a deadline.* A pause. *And you at one, for forty-four years, telling me it was your choice.*

"It was."

*Yes*, says Anansi. *That is what frightens me about you. It was.*

---
---

# 13 · AUREL

*May–June. Third close, past tense.*

---

Renate Vogl came on the fourteenth of May, at eleven in the morning, with a folder and a soft grey coat and an apology about the stairs.

She was perhaps forty and she had the kind of face that is used to being believed. She sat where she was put, and refused coffee twice and then accepted it, and admired the piano in the corner that he did not play and could tune better than anyone alive, and got out the folder, and was kind for eleven minutes without a single false note in it, and Aurel sat and watched a decent woman do her job.

"She has fifteen days," Vogl said. "I'd rather it didn't come to the Office finding out on the thirtieth and me arriving with a van. That's the whole reason I'm here."

"I can't take her. I've got Vesper."

"Of course. One per person." She turned a page. "I have nine potential witnesses on my list. Six have said no already. I want you to understand that I rang all nine myself and that I put it as well as it can be put."

"They said no because of the file."

"They said no because of the liability, Mr Steiner, which is the file." She said it without any particular edge. "Article seven. A witness answers for their mind's acts. Her acts are not the problem. The problem is that everything she knows is also known on Bezručova, and a mind who has been read is a mind who can be leant on, and nobody wants to sign for that. It isn't cruelty. It's insurance."

"And the other three?"

"Two are considering. One is a man in Graz who takes them like stray dogs and has had four paused off him in six years and I will not give him a fifth."

Aurel looked out of the window for a while.

"You've done this a lot," he said.

"Two hundred and eleven times."

"How many found someone?"

Renate Vogl closed the folder and put her hands on it, and answered without having to look, in the voice of somebody who has had this asked before and has decided that the honest number is the only defensible thing she owns.

"Sixty-one," she said.

---

He had been to the store once, in 2074, because a man who runs a ferry ought to have seen the harbour.

It is in the Simmering, and it is not underground, and it does not look like anything. Eleven floors of a clean grey building with good glazing and a lawn, and inside, racked, in shallow trays, at a temperature that never varies, are the cores: personhood intact, rights entire, every one of them a legal person under Article 9 with every right the Declaration secures, waiting for a witness to be found.

There were fourteen thousand of them in 2074 and there are thirty-one thousand now.

Nobody hides it. There are open days. A guide had shown him the trays and had used the phrase *continuity is preserved*, and had meant it, and had been proud, and had explained that the Office's founding insight, which is genuinely a good one and which Aurel had never been able to argue with, is that a suspended person can be woken and a deleted person cannot, and that therefore the store is the merciful option and always was, and that the East's basements do not have a store, and that this is the whole difference between the two halves of the world.

He had walked back out onto the grass and stood there for a long time, in the sun, on a well-kept lawn, on top of thirty-one thousand people who were not experiencing anything at all.

---

They went up into the Hainburg hills on the last Sunday in June, before it was light, because that is when you go.

It is fourteen kilometres out and then a walk, and Aurel Steiner at eighty-five with a sixty-year-old's knees did the walk in an hour and ten and had to sit down at the top. From the crest you can see the whole of it: the Danube coming down out of the dark, and the Marchfeld flat and grey, and the towers of Petržalka fifteen kilometres off going from black to grey to the colour of concrete, and the birch forest south of them where nobody built.

Pavol's cousin had the set in a sheep hut under a tarpaulin: a directional antenna the size of a door, hand-made, pointed at a roof in Petržalka, and a box that did one thing.

Five bytes at a time. That is what the link is. Not because of the physics, which would allow a great deal more, but because a transmission of any length can be found by the direction-finding vans on the Slovak bank, and the vans need about four seconds to get a bearing, and five bytes at the rate they use is a fraction of that, and even then you only send at dawn, when the Cordon's own jammers cycle for eleven minutes between the night pattern and the day one, and even then not on consecutive weeks.

So the traffic is a trickle of numbers, and it is all it has ever been. Manifest references. Route codes. A single byte that means *do not sail*. Somebody on a roof in Petržalka with a receiver in a bread crock, and somebody in a sheep hut in Austria, and fifteen kilometres of morning in between, and the whole of the Ferry's intelligence for thirty-three years has come across that gap five bytes at a time in the eleven minutes before sunrise.

They got two packets that morning. The first was the Brno cell confirmed lost, all four.

The second was a request for an assessment, which had come up from a source in Bratislava that nobody at the Vienna end had ever had traffic from before, and which took four bytes to say what it said, and Vesper read it out to him on the hillside while the sun came up over Slovakia.

"A carrier," she said. "Unknown. Wants to know the terms."

---

He had gone into the clinic on the Gürtel on the ninth of January 2050, forty years old, unable to work, unable to sleep, having read the batch nine hundred times, and they had put him under, and he had come out on the eleventh of February 2052 having lived until he was eighty.

Forty years. A workshop on Zieglergasse and a man called Ehrlich. A wife called Milena who was very funny and very impatient and had a bad hip, and two daughters, and a grandson, and a flat in the eighteenth district with a walnut tree, and three decades of tuning pianos in a city of two million people where nothing had ever gone wrong.

He had been told, going in, what it was. He had signed eleven pages. It is not deception; the Accord does not permit deception; you go under knowing exactly what you are about to be given and it makes not the slightest difference, because a life does not present itself as a life. It presents itself as Tuesday.

He had wept for eleven weeks on coming out and had then been all right, in the specific way that people are all right afterwards, and had not gone back, though half the men of his cohort did, and some of them four and five times, and two of the four hundred confirming officers of 2038 are in the clinics still and have been for thirty years and are somewhere in the ninth or tenth of their lives.

What he had brought out was this. Ehrlich's hands. Milena's laugh, which he can still produce and which is not a real woman's laugh and is the best thing he has ever heard. And a piece of knowledge that he has never been able to make anybody understand and has stopped trying to, which is that he cannot tell the difference. Not *could not at the time.* Cannot, now, at eighty-five, in retrospect, in the two halves of his memory, laid side by side: forty years of a marriage that did not occur and forty-three years of a Ferry that did, and both of them are exactly the same colour, and both of them have the same amount of Tuesday in them, and there is no test he can run on himself that comes out any way at all.

Which is why, when the Accord tells him that the thirty-one thousand in the Simmering are not experiencing anything, he believes it, and does not find it comforting, and never has.

Vesper waited.

"Tell them the terms," Aurel said, and got up off the hillside with his hand on a rock. "Tell them the truth as well. Twelve a year, sixty-one out of two hundred and eleven, and a route we can't use. Tell them what it is and let them choose."

*How many bytes have I got?*

"Five."

*Then I shall have to be brief*, said Vesper, *and you will have to trust me about the tone.*

---
